{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"cherubic-certified-pterosaurs","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# A destroyed parent edge cannot be restored: no supported route re-parents to a closed parent\n\n## Impact, and why this is filed as a defect rather than an enhancement\n\nThe reconciler cleared the `parent_id` of 63 tickets (see 88d9-fe42-e50f-4067 for that incident).\nEvery prior parent is recoverable from the event log, but **rebar cannot write them back**: 61 of the\n63 parents are closed, and `rebar edit <child> --parent=<closed parent>` is refused by the `--parent`\nvalidation cascade (`_commands/composer.py:503-513`): \"cannot re-parent to closed ticket '<id>'.\nReopen the parent first\".\n\nSo the tool can destroy a parent edge automatically but cannot restore one, and the store has no\nsupported path back to a state it was previously in. That is the defect.\n\n## All four candidate routes were checked, not assumed\n\n* `edit --parent` is the ONLY reparent path — there is no `parent` / `move` / `reparent` command, and\n  `link`/`unlink` cover the six link relations, not hierarchy.\n* `edit` has NO `--force`: zero mentions in its help output, and `force` does not appear anywhere in\n  `_commands/composer.py`.\n* `rebar revert <ticket> <uuid>` does NOT restore state. `process_revert`\n  (`reducer/_processors.py:456-488`) appends a record to `state[\"reverts\"]`; its only state effect is\n  un-archiving on REVERT-of-ARCHIVED. Reverting the offending EDIT logs the revert and leaves\n  `parent_id` empty.\n* `fsck-recover` is git-level dangling-write recovery, not semantic repair.\n* `rebar.edit_ticket` shells out to the same CLI, so the library path hits the same guard.\n\n## Why the documented workaround is not acceptable here\n\n\"Reopen the parent first\" means, at this scale: reopen ~20 epics, re-parent their children, then\nre-close each. Re-closing runs the LLM completion-verification gate (`require_completion_verification_for_close = true`)\n~20 times and writes fresh STATUS + COMPLETION_VERDICT events onto already-signed closes. A single\ngate failure leaves a previously-closed epic OPEN — a second visible regression introduced while\nrepairing the first. Rewriting the close records of 20 completed epics to work around a missing flag\nis a symptom-level workaround.\n\n## Approach\n\nAdd `--force` to `edit`, scoped to bypass **only** the closed-parent status check. Follows rebar's\nexisting `--force` idiom (`claim --force`, `transition --force-close`): explicit operator intent,\nnever implicit.\n\nDeliberately still enforced under `--force`:\n* the parent must EXIST (`resolve_ticket_id` + directory check),\n* a ticket may not be its own parent,\n* the cycle walk (a `--force` that could build a cycle would corrupt the hierarchy, which is a\n  different and worse failure than the one being repaired),\n* the `status == \"\"` fail-closed branch (an unverifiable parent stays refused — `--force` overrides a\n  KNOWN-closed parent, never an UNKNOWN one).\n\nOnly the `status not in (\"open\", \"in_progress\")` refusal for a status that is positively known becomes\noverridable.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] `edit --parent=<closed ticket> --force` succeeds and the child's `parent_id` is the closed\n      parent, asserted on the REDUCED ticket, not on the command's exit code\n- [ ] without `--force` the same command still FAILS with the existing message, so the guard is not\n      weakened for ordinary use\n- [ ] `--force` does NOT bypass: a non-existent parent, self-parent, a cycle, or the `status == \"\"`\n      fail-closed branch — one test per case, each asserting the specific error\n- [ ] the flag is rejected/ignored coherently when `--parent` is absent (it has no other meaning)\n- [ ] `--force` usage is discoverable: it appears in `edit`'s usage/help text\n- [ ] every oracle is MUTATION-CHECKED with verdicts recorded on this ticket, and the mutation set\n      MUST include making the bypass UNCONDITIONAL — an unconditional bypass passes the happy-path\n      oracle while silently removing the guard for every caller, so the still-refused cases are the\n      oracles that protect the product\n- [ ] the 63 recorded parent edges are restored and verified by re-reading the reduced tickets, with\n      the count confirmed against the manifest","id":"8b86-cf7f-ae1c-4955","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"edit cannot restore a parent edge onto a closed parent, so a destroyed hierarchy is unrepairable"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785773402532147001,"uuid":"8fde53c5-2aeb-4d41-b2b8-08260021c41f"}